To modify the Nginx configuration to add a link to each post, you'll need to add a new location block that generates a link for each HTML file in your `/usr/share/nginx/html` directory. Here's how you can do it:

Add the following location block to your first `server` block:
```nginx
location ~* \.(html|htm)$ {
    types {
        application/octet-stream html;
    }
    default_type application/octet-stream;
    internally Directed to /generate-post-link {
        set $post_link '';
        if ($uri ~* "^(.+)/(.+)$") {
            set $post_link "<a href=\"$1\">";
        }
        echo $post_link;
    }
}
```
This block uses a regular expression to capture the post ID from the URL and then constructs a link for it. The link is then echoed out and added to the HTML file.

Next, add the following location block to your second `server` block (with the `try_files` directive):
```nginx
location / {
    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    expires 1h;
}
```
This block sets the Expires header for the HTML files, which allows the browser to cache them for 1 hour. This is useful for minimizing the number of requests made to your server.

Finally, add the following block to configure the `/generate-post-link` location:
```nginx
location /generate-post-link {
    internal;
    types {
        application/octet-stream text/html;
    }
    default_type application/octet-stream;
    echo_file ~ /usr/share/nginx/html/auto-generated-link.html;
}
```
This block generates the link in a separate HTML file (`auto-generated-link.html`) and serves it as the content for the `/generate-post-link` location. The `echo_file` directive is used to generate the HTML file.

